From 8a85e680e1a66404e4f4b65c23a9ebb482fa7d3b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lucascouts <lucascs@protonmail.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 06:40:42 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] sidebar, agent_ui: a thread pins to its project as well as
 globally
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Zeo next proposal §6.5 (ask 12): a pin is global, as 0049 made it, or
belongs to the thread's project. A project pin heads its project's
group in the threads sidebar; the Pinned section keeps the global
ones.

The pin itself -- whether a thread is pinned, and the order of pins --
stays where 0048 keeps it. Only the scope is new, and it lives in a
database domain of its own (ThreadPinScopeDb, one table,
thread_pin_scope) for the reason 0096 gives: 0048's migration list is
appended to by 0063 under a USE flag, so a step appended there by an
earlier patch would sit at a different index with and without the
flag. A row means "pinned to its project"; no row means global. The
project is the thread's own, read from its metadata when the sidebar
groups it, so it is not stored twice. Like the organization, the scope
belongs to a saved thread (admit) and goes with a deleted one (forget).

In the sidebar, pinned_section::pinned_thread_ids leaves project pins
out of the Pinned section, so their group lists them like any thread,
and project_pins::hoist_project_pins then moves them to the top of the
group in pin order -- after the group's sort and 0048's manual order,
which would otherwise undo it. The thread menu offers "Pin to Project"
to an unpinned thread, before "Pin Thread" so the entries after that
one keep their place; a pinned thread still offers "Unpin Thread"
alone, whichever its scope. The scope is written only after the
organization accepted the toggle: a refused toggle changes nothing and
still warns once. An unpin clears the scope, so a later global pin
does not inherit it.

Tests: pin_scope::tests (a migration list of its own; a project pin
survives a restart and global clears it; a draft has none; a deleted
thread's is forgotten), project_pins::tests (project pins go first in
pin order and the rest keep theirs; absent pins and threadless rows
are left alone) and sidebar_pinned_tests::project_pins (a project pin
heads its group and skips the Pinned section; unpinning clears the
scope). Seen failing under three probes: the restart, the hoist and
the unpin's scope.
---

